    <!-- EDITORIAL CONTENT -->
    <section class="editorial-section">
      <div class="container editorial-content">
        <h2>Understanding the 8th Central Pay Commission: Evolution &amp; Projections</h2>
        <p>
          The constitution of the 8th Central Pay Commission represents a major fiscal milestone for over 4.9 million central government employees and more than 6.5 million pensioners across India. In accordance with the historical 10-year convention established by the Central Government, pay commissions are instituted every decade to review, rationalize, and elevate basic pay scales, allowance frameworks, and pension dispensations in response to inflation and macroeconomic growth.
        </p>

        <div class="info-card">
          <h4>Why January 1, 2026 is the Crucial Benchmark Date</h4>
          <p>
            The 6th CPC took effect on January 1, 2006, and the 7th CPC became effective on January 1, 2016. In line with this decade-long interval, the 8th Central Pay Commission recommendations are expected to take effect from <strong>January 1, 2026</strong>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <h3 id="fitment-factor">The Fitment Factor Debate: 1.92 vs 2.57 vs 2.86 vs 3.68</h3>
        <p>
          The cornerstone of any Pay Commission's recommendations is the <strong>fitment factor</strong>. The fitment factor is the uniform numerical multiplier applied to existing basic pay to calculate the revised basic pay under the newly drafted pay matrix.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Conservative Projection (1.92x):</strong> Reflects inflation neutralization if accumulated Dearness Allowance (DA) is absorbed with a modest real wage growth markup. Minimum basic pay would rise from ₹18,000 to approximately ₹34,560.</li>
          <li><strong>7th CPC Baseline Benchmark (2.57x):</strong> The factor recommended by the Justice A.K. Mathur commission in the 7th CPC. Applying this benchmark elevates minimum wage to ₹46,260.</li>
          <li><strong>Staff Side / Union Demand (2.86x – 3.68x):</strong> National employee joint consultative bodies argue that soaring housing, education, and healthcare costs warrant a higher multiplier to match the Aykroyd formula for minimum nutritional and living standards.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>DA Merger &amp; House Rent Allowance (HRA) Rationalization</h3>
        <p>
          When a new pay commission is formally adopted, the accumulated Dearness Allowance percentage (which reached 50% in early 2024 and continues rising) is reset back to zero. The accumulated purchasing power relief is permanently folded into the expanded basic pay. Subsequently, House Rent Allowance (HRA) undergoes categorization adjustments:
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>X-Class Cities (Metros):</strong> Revised basic pay attracts 30% HRA (subject to periodic upward revisions as DA crosses prescribed milestones).</li>
          <li><strong>Y-Class Cities (Tier 2 Municipalities):</strong> Compensated with 20% HRA.</li>
          <li><strong>Z-Class Cities (Towns &amp; Rural Stations):</strong> Compensated with 10% HRA.</li>
        </ol>

        <!-- FAQS -->
        <div class="faq-block" id="faqs">
