          <div class="faq-item">
            <div class="faq-question">When will the 8th Pay Commission be implemented?</div>
            <div class="faq-answer">
              Following the established 10-year periodic review cycle (6th CPC in 2006, 7th CPC in 2016), the 8th Central Pay Commission is expected to take effect from January 1, 2026.
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="faq-item">
            <div class="faq-question">What is the formula to calculate 8th CPC revised basic pay?</div>
            <div class="faq-answer">
              The standard formula is: <code>8th CPC Basic Pay = Current 7th CPC Basic Pay × Approved Fitment Factor</code>, followed by index-matching to the nearest cell in the newly published Pay Matrix.
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="faq-item">
            <div class="faq-question">How are pensioners affected by the 8th Pay Commission?</div>
            <div class="faq-answer">
              Under central parity principles, pensions are revised using the same fitment factor. Basic pension is computed as 50% of the revised notional basic pay, accompanied by revised Dearness Relief (DR).
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="faq-item">
            <div class="faq-question">Is 8paycommission.com an official government portal?</div>
            <div class="faq-answer">
              No. 8paycommission.com is an independent educational and financial calculation utility designed to help employees and pensioners estimate future payouts based on past commission precedents and official union proposals.
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

      </div>
